During the life of a process it may enter into different states.

State of process
----------------

-> Ready state
-> Running state
-> Wait state
-> Delay state
-> Suspended state


Ready state
-----------

	If the process is in ready state it needs CPU but nothing. Once it dispatched to CPU then it is in running state.

Running state
-------------

	Process is executed by CPU.

Wait state
----------

	waiting for an external event to finish. 
	let us consider scanf(). 

	new process ----------> ready ----------> Running
                     admitted         dispatcher

	during run time if it waiting for external events then it goes to wait state and if events completed then it goes to ready state. when the cpu is completed other tasks then again dispatcher dispatches to running state. 

Delayed State
-------------
	
	process intentionally goes to delay state (by providing sleep()). 

new -> ready --------------> CPU  
	^	dispatcher    |
	|		      |
	| delay complted		  
	|		     delay
	______________________|



dispatcher is a module in process manager it gives control of the CPU to the process.

Suspended State 
---------------

During the life of a process it is possible to suspend the process by sending signal.

kill -19 process_id for suspend the process
kill -18 process_id for resume the process
			
			dispatcher
new ---------> ready  --------------->  (p1) CPU
     admitted	  ^			  |
		  | 			  |  kill -19 pid
     kill -18 pid |  resume		  |
		  |_______________________|
			  suspended


Note : when any process receiving signal 9 then process is terminate...


CPU bound vs I/O bound
----------------------

* A process in its life time always demands the CPU, that process is called CPU bound process

	main() {

		pf("hello");
		while(1);	// always demanding CPU that is normally CPU bound process.
	}


	
	_________________
	|		|		      __________ CPU
	|  p1	   p2	|    dispatcher	     |		|	
	|  p3  		|------------------> |	p1  p2	|  (10 ms)
	| 		|		     |	  p3	|	timeslice
	-----------------		     ------------

But in Software multiprocessing there should be time slice... so CPU bound process get the timeslice time only...


I/O bound
---------

	A process in its life time always not required the CPU. Those Jobs are called I/O bound process. 

	ex : shell

	According process manager I/O bound jobs are preferable.


